***Abstract.***

*It* *will* be *argue*d *here* that the *multi*faceted condition of digitality- the social, technocratic, institutional, cultural, spatial, temporal universe; cyberspace; projected by communicative capitalism- the reification of democratic values in(/into) networked communications *technologique* (Dean, 2005)-- is the most important change in contemporary European societies. This essay will engage with qualitative academic literature, alongside critical discourse analysis of digital journalism with heads of state, relevant political/corporate actors, more importantly the *demos* themselves, involving Albania, France and wider world. Through further qualitative engagement with historical, institutional, but essentially (digital;) *cultural* and *social* phenomena and ensuing perspectives we ascertain shifting- but uncannily familiar and incongruent, perpetually acrid with anxiety-induction- ‘*haunting*’- ideological perspectives of the European polity; With regards to the bottom-up: Demographic trends, (digitally accelerated) cultural *inertia* **but first media, digital, felt manifestations- ideal and actual- in both the individual and cultural sensibility, extra-parliamentary public discourse and grassroots mobilisation will be considered. The theoretical approach taken here (especially) with regard to assessing the impacts on politics and Europe will be, regarding economic, institutional, spatial issues (but also certain oracular social insights), from and building on (neo-)Marxist strains of thought; Where we shall venture for the corpus of our (cybernetic/) cultural insight will herald Hassan’s (T*he Condition of Digitality: A Post-Modern Marxism for the Practice of Digital Life*, ch.7, 2020) counsel that Marxism should go Post-modern and therefore engage with Nietzsche, Baudrillard, Foucault, Fisher’s exploded big Other, hauntology, amongst other concomitant strains of thought and interdisciplinary insights whilst proposing a radical theory of AI and what it means for society, the future (or lack thereof). This essay intentionally dreams a wide pool of political theory and interdisciplinary literature in explanatory weight for the ‘perpetually revolutionary’ (Harvey, pp.188, 1989) trends that we will compare and argue are ubiquitously experienced, sensed and felt in light of digitality through a Foucauldian (1980) love of plurality and examination of the human body as the only irreducible by virtue of being the site where repression is ultimately registered (Harvey, pp.45, 1989): And thereby practices an imaginal, sensual, hermeneutic, cultural, cybernetic, anthropological approach to understanding the phenomenon of digitality and its effects on politics, life in Europe, and the wider world. 

